@@ -0,0 +1,14 @@
+@tier:static @pillar:compliance @awaiting:EVID-01
+Feature: Every prod promotion leaves a release record
+  A-23: reporting and governance documentation is a by-product of delivery. Each promotion
+  records who changed what, under which spec, with which tests and evidence, and when.
+  It is a GitHub Release with an attestation now; S3 Object Lock in a log-archive account
+  is planned (catalog: release-record). Contract: DC-010.
+
+  @CTL-060 @nist:CM-3 @ssdf:PS.3.1 @env:prod
+  Scenario: The prod promotion publishes and attests a release record
+    Given the delivery workflows
+    Then at least one job deploys to "prod"
+    And every job that deploys to "prod" writes the release record with "scripts/ci/release-record" after it deploys
+    And every job that deploys to "prod" publishes the release record as a GitHub Release
+    And every job that deploys to "prod" attests the release record with predicate type "https://github.com/dgaspard/ai-sdlc-specc-bdd-design/release-record/v1"
diff --color -ruN a/spec/delivery/features/infrastructure/oidc-trust.feature b/spec/delivery/features/infrastructure/oidc-trust.feature
--- a/spec/delivery/features/infrastructure/oidc-trust.feature	1969-12-31 18:00:00
+++ b/spec/delivery/features/infrastructure/oidc-trust.feature	2026-10-10 16:05:40
@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+@tier:template @pillar:security @awaiting:ENV-02
+Feature: GitHub can assume an AWS role only for its own environment
+  OIDC lets a workflow swap a short-lived GitHub token for AWS credentials. Whoever can
+  edit a workflow decides which jobs ask, so the role's trust policy is the real boundary
+  (A-21): this repository, one GitHub environment, nothing broader.
+  Catalog: aws-oidc.
